From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:22:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Poster: GMod's poster command rendered in tiles of the
 window's size

GMod's `poster` renders a picture several times the window's size by drawing tiles through sheared
(off-centre) projections and reading back the raster image. The camera manager rejects a shear above
0.01, so under RTX none of the tiles was path traced. The runtime now renders the poster itself.

- rtxposter_Start/Cancel/GetStatus (d3d9.dll exports, rtx_poster_api.h): a multiplier of 1 to 16,
  frames per tile, the PNG's path, and an optional copy of each tile.
  They reach the device's poster through the poster's own instance (rtx_poster_instance.h): GMod never
  registers its D3D9 device with the Remix API, so the API's device pointer stays empty.
- While a poster renders, the main, view model and sky cameras' projections are narrowed to one
  tile's window of the game's own view (rtx_poster_math.h). Their field of view and aspect stay the
  game's, so the culling frustum and the view model's detection keep the whole view. Every tile has
  the window's size: no upscaler, denoiser or render target changes size. Neighbouring tiles overlap by
  a sixteenth of the window's short side (16 to 256 pixels) and are blended over it.
- A tile's first frame resets the history; its last frame (64 by default), the picture before its UI,
  is blitted to RGBA8 and read back through the exporter. The tiles are composed in reading order into
  8-bit BGR and written on a thread of their own with Windows' PNG encoder (WIC through ole32 at run
  time; nothing new is linked), as <path>.part and then renamed.
- Meanwhile the runtime's clock stands still (GlobalTime), exposure keeps its value, and motion blur
  and the lens effects (vignette, chromatic aberration) are left out. The physical sky's cloud layer
  and aerial perspective, both screen-aligned, are fitted to the tile's window; the sky matte's
  viewport is stretched so the tile's window covers it, its jitter shrunk to stay a pixel; textures
  and the sky are sampled for the poster's pixel size.
- It stops when the view moves, the window changes size, VR starts or it is cancelled. 16384 x 16384
  at most (the stretched matte viewport stays within every GPU's limit).

tests/rtx/unit/test_poster_math.cpp (764 checks) covers the layout, the narrowed projections and
direction bases, and the composer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
